Deep Dive: Hashgate Vulnerability Mechanics
The Hashgate challenge by Yahaya Meddy is a textbook example of why obscurity is not security.  Here is the detailed technical breakdown of the exploitation chain:

1. The Core Flaw: Predictable Object References
The application uses sequential integers for User IDs (e.g., 3000, 3001, 3012). While it attempts to hide these IDs by converting them to MD5 hashes in the URL, the underlying data remains highly predictable. 

Your Session: You log in as guest (ID 3000).
URL: .../profile/user/e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d
Math: MD5("3000") = e93028bdc1aacdfb3687181f2031765d
The Assumption: The developers assumed that because the ID is hashed, an attacker cannot guess the URL for other users. 
The Reality: Since the ID space is small (integers) and sequential, an attacker can simply generate hashes for every number in a specific range (e.g., 3000–3020) and try them all. 

